<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Nasser</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> is a reservoir on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>, completed in 1970. More than 500 kilometres long, it flooded much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, displacing about 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> and prompting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> campaign that moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and other monuments and rescued the paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> cathedral. The Sudanese part is called Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-nasser.jpg" alt="Lake Nasser" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Nasser_Vista.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
